---
export const prerender = true;

import RoomLayout from '../../../layouts/RoomLayout.astro';
import ZybachNav from '../../../components/zybach/ZybachNav.astro';
import CitationBlock from '../../../components/zybach/CitationBlock.astro';
import { RIGHTS_STATEMENT } from '../../../components/zybach/_data';
import { monographs } from '../../../components/zybach/soapcreek/data';
import { citationLabel, chapterHref, folioHref } from '../../../components/zybach/soapcreek/links';

/* The worked example is a real record, printed verbatim — never a specimen
   written for this page. #05 is the one the Series Index cites most. */
const example = monographs.find((m) => String(m.number) === '5') ?? monographs[0] ?? null;
const exampleCanonical = example?.citation_canonical ?? '';
const exampleShort = example?.citation_short ?? '';
const examplePage = 13;
const exampleHref = example ? folioHref(example.slug, examplePage) : null;
const exampleShorthand = example ? citationLabel(example.number as any, examplePage) : '';
---

<RoomLayout
  room="collections"
  title="How to cite the Zybach Collection"
  subtitle="Cite the book the way Bob prints it. Cite this page when you mean this page."
  width="prose"
  sourceId="collections-zybach-how-to-cite"
  parents={[
    { label: 'The Zybach Collection', href: '/collections/zybach/' },
  ]}
  noindex={true}
>
  <span data-pagefind-meta="collection:zybach" hidden></span>

  <div class="source-page">
    <ZybachNav current="how-to-cite" />

    <p class="provisional-note">
      <strong>Preview — under review by the creator; not yet public.</strong>
      Page addresses in this preview are intended to be permanent, but they are
      not guaranteed until the collection is published.
    </p>

    <p>
      Bob Zybach's series has cited itself the same way since 1993, and that
      habit is the most useful thing in it. Keep using it. The Library's
      addresses exist to get you to the page, not to replace the citation.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rule">The first rule</h2>

    <p>
      <strong>Cite the source, not the website.</strong> If you are quoting a
      narrator, you are quoting a printed monograph; cite the monograph and the
      printed page. Add the Library's address after it if you want a reader to
      be able to click through, the way you would add a DOI.
    </p>

    <h2 id="monographs">Soap Creek monographs</h2>

    <p>
      Every monograph carries a canonical citation in its own front matter. The
      Library prints that string verbatim at the top and bottom of each item
      page. Copy it as printed — including the monograph number, which is how
      the series' own index refers to it.
    </p>

    {exampleCanonical && (
      <>
        <p class="zc-example">{exampleCanonical}</p>
        <p class="zc-caption">
          That is the string monograph #{example!.number} prints in its own front
          matter, reproduced here character for character — spacing and all. It
          is the same string the item page prints under "As printed".
        </p>
      </>
    )}

    <h3 id="passage">Citing one page of it</h3>

    <p>
      For a passage, name the printed page and add the series' own shorthand,
      which is monograph number, colon, page. Every item page prints this line
      for you, made from the page you are looking at:
    </p>

    {exampleShort && (
      <p class="zc-example">
        <strong>{exampleShort}, p. {examplePage} — Monograph #{exampleShorthand}</strong>
        <br />
        {exampleHref
          ? `https://schooltrusts.org${exampleHref}`
          : `https://schooltrusts.org/collections/zybach/soap-creek/${example?.slug}/`}
      </p>
    )}

    <p>
      The shorthand — <strong>{exampleShorthand || '05:13'}</strong> — is what
      Bob's master index has used since 1993, so a reader with the printed books
      and a reader with this site are citing the same thing.
    </p>

    <h2 id="page-anchors">Page addresses</h2>

    <p>
      Printed page numbers are the citation unit for the whole series, so they
      are the citation unit here too. Every printed page begins its own block of
      text with its page number in the margin, and that page number is a link. A
      reference like <strong>05:47</strong> resolves to the chapter of monograph
      #5 that contains printed page 47, ending in <code>#p47</code> — the
      permanent address of that one printed page.
    </p>

    <p class="zc-example">
      https://schooltrusts.org/collections/zybach/soap-creek/m05-rohner/&lt;chapter&gt;/#p47
    </p>

    <p>
      Click the little "p. 47" in the margin and the address in your browser bar
      is that page's permanent link. Send it to anyone.
    </p>

    <p>
      Front matter numbered in roman numerals keeps its roman page numbers and is
      addressed separately, so <code>#pv</code> and <code>#p5</code> are
      different pages.
    </p>

    <h2 id="elliott">Elliott interviews, recordings and videos</h2>

    <p>
      The Elliott material is indexed the way Bob indexed it in 2019: by
      interview date, tape side, minute, and transcript page. Cite all four when
      you can.
    </p>

    <p class="zc-example">
      Phillips, Jerry. Interviewed by Bob Zybach, 10 October 2017, Elliott State
      Forest field tour. Tape 3-B at 4:15; transcript p. 97. Elliott State
      Forest Oral History Collection, Oregon Websites and Watersheds Project.
    </p>

    <p>
      For a video, cite the video and the time code, as its own description
      does: title, channel, date, and the minute. The Library's video pages link
      each time code back to the same moment.
    </p>

    <h2 id="thesis">Thesis and Auto Tour</h2>

    <p class="zc-example">
      Zybach, Bob. 1999. <em>Using Oral Histories to Document Changing Forest
      Cover Patterns: Soap Creek Valley, Oregon, 1500–1999</em>. M.A.I.S.
      thesis, Oregon State University. 321 pp.
    </p>

    <p class="zc-example">
      Zybach, Bob. 1989. <em>Historic Soap Creek Valley Auto Tour</em>.
      Corvallis: Oregon State University Research Forests. 32 pp.
    </p>

    <h2 id="tapes">If you need the tape</h2>

    <p>
      The recordings are held at the OSU Special Collections and Archives
      Research Center. Cite them by collection number and interview date — OH
      006 for Soap Creek, OH 047 for the Elliott — and request them from SCARC.
      Two Soap Creek interviews (Rawie and Davies) have no extant cassette; for
      those the printed transcript is the only record.
    </p>

    <h2 id="this-collection">The collection as a whole</h2>

    <CitationBlock
      canonical="The Zybach Collection. America's School Trust Library, 2026. Finding aid."
      short="The Zybach Collection, finding aid."
      url="/collections/zybach/"
      accessed="2026"
    />

    <h2 id="rights">Reuse</h2>

    <p>{RIGHTS_STATEMENT}</p>

    <p>
      SCARC's finding-aid pages carry the Center's standard footer, "This work is
      licensed under a Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International License."
      That covers SCARC's own description of the collections. It is not a licence
      on the recordings, the transcripts or the photographs.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">If a citation here is wrong</h2>

    <p>
      Say so. The Library corrects in public: the old wording, the new wording,
      and the reason all stay visible in the
      <a href="/about/corrections/">corrections ledger</a>. A wrong page number
      in an index is worth reporting; it propagates.
    </p>

    <p class="see-also">
      See also: <a href="/about/cite/">How to cite the Library</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/provenance/">Provenance</a> ·
      <a href="/collections/zybach/">Finding aid</a>
    </p>
  </div>
</RoomLayout>

<style>
  .zc-example {
    margin: 0.9rem 0;
    padding: 0.7rem 1rem;
    background: var(--linen-card, #fffdf7);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--old-gold, #a87f2c);
    border-radius: 3px;
    font-size: 0.94rem;
    line-height: 1.55;
    overflow-wrap: anywhere;
  }
</style>
